@database "session_archive"
@author "Enrico Ehrich"
@$VER: session_archive.guide 1.0 (29.09.2026)
@smartwrap

@node main "Session Archive"
@{h1}Session Archive

Old conversations do not stay in the live session store forever. Once a
conversation and every sub-agent session it started have been left alone
long enough, the archive sweep packs them into one zip file and takes them
out of the store -- out of the sidebar, out of every listing. The
@{b}Session Archive@{ub} panel shows what was put away and brings a
conversation back, whole, so it can be continued. You can also delete an
archived conversation for good, or run the sweep by hand.

The archive itself belongs to the app; this plugin is its panel and the
HTTP routes behind it. It gives agents no tools.

@{image docs/panel.png "The Session Archive panel with three archived conversations"}

@{code}
For users
  @{"  The Session Archive panel  " link panel}
  @{"  What moves, and when  " link sweep}

For developers and administrators
  @{"  The HTTP routes  " link api}
  @{"  On the command line  " link cli}
  @{"  Setting it up  " link setup}
@{body}
@endnode

@node panel "The Session Archive panel"
@{h1}The Session Archive panel

Open it from the launcher (@{b}Session@{ub}, "Session Archive").

@{image docs/panel.png "The Session Archive panel"}

@{h2}Whose archive it shows

Your own: every route answers about the user who is logged in, and there
is no way to ask for anyone else's archive. With login switched off,
everybody is @{tt}anonymous@{utt} and shares that one archive. The panel
does not follow the chat; it always shows all of your archived
conversations. It does not reload on its own: the reload button loads
at once, and the menu next to it turns a reload every 60 seconds (or
another interval) on, which the browser remembers.

@{h2}What you see

@{bullet}Four figures: how many conversations are archived, how many
sessions they hold together, the size of their zip files on disk, and
after how many days a conversation is archived.
@{bullet}A table, one row per conversation: its title with the id under
it, the agent it ran with, its number of sessions, the size of its zip,
when it was last used and when it was archived. The newest archive comes
first. A click on a column head sorts by it, a second click the other way;
the order holds through the reloads. Size and the two dates sort by the
exact value, not by the rounded text -- a sweep archives many
conversations within the same minute.
@{bullet}With nothing archived, the panel says when conversations move
here -- or, when the periodic sweep is switched off, that nothing moves
until you press @{b}Archive now@{ub}.

@{h2}What you can do

@{table}
Button | Does
Restore (arrow) | puts the conversation and all its sub-agent sessions back; it is in the sidebar again and can be continued
Delete (bin) | deletes the archived conversation, after asking; there is no copy after this
Archive now | runs the sweep for your own conversations at once, instead of waiting for the next periodic one
@{body}

While a restore or a delete is on its way, both buttons of that row stay
off. The answer is a message at the bottom: "restored (5 sessions)",
"deleted", "Archived 2 conversation(s), 7 sessions", "Nothing was old
enough to archive", or "Nothing archived: 1 conversation(s) old enough are
in use or failed". When a pass stops at the configured cap, the message
says how many are still waiting, and pressing the button again takes the
next batch.

@{b}A restored conversation keeps its old timestamps.@{ub} If you do not
continue it, it is still old enough, and the next sweep -- the periodic
one or @{b}Archive now@{ub} -- archives it again. Write a message in it to
keep it in the store.

@{h2}When the server refuses

The server's reason is shown as a message and the list is loaded anew:

@{bullet}A restore is refused when a session of the conversation is in the
store again as something else (it would be overwritten), when one of its
session ids now belongs to another user, or when the archive holds
sessions of another user.
@{bullet}"a sweep for ... is already running": another pass for you is
under way, in this process or in another one (a command-line run).
@{bullet}"the archive index of ... is held by another process": a sweep of
another process is writing the index; try again in a moment.
@{bullet}"Session archive is not available": the app runs without the
archive service.

When the list itself cannot be loaded, the panel says "The archive could
not be read" with the reason, and shows no figures.
@endnode

@node sweep "What moves, and when"
@{h1}What moves, and when

@{h2}A whole conversation at a time

What is archived is a tree: a conversation together with every sub-agent
session below it, because half a conversation is worth nothing. A tree
moves only when @{b}every@{ub} session in it is older than the retention
(30 days as shipped) and @{b}none@{ub} of them is in use. The age is the
session's last change as the session index records it; without one, the
file's modification time. A session whose age cannot be read at all
counts as touched just now.

"In use" is asked right before each tree is taken: a session a run of the
API is working on, or one another process holds a session lock for. A
command-line process knows no runs of the API; there the locks are the only
guard -- and with @{tt}session_presence@{utt} switched off there are none,
so only the age protects a session (the command line warns about it).

@{h2}The order

For each tree: write the zip, read it back, enter it in the user's archive
index, and only then delete the live files -- the deepest sub-agent session
first, the conversation itself last. A crash in between leaves a duplicate,
never a lost conversation. A zip that is already there is only added to,
never replaced. A session that cannot be deleted is reported and stays;
the next pass adds whatever is left to the same zip.

@{h2}When it runs

The periodic sweep starts 5 minutes after the app has started and then
runs every 24 hours, for every user in the store. It is switched on as
shipped (see @{" Setting it up " link setup}). @{b}Archive now@{ub} in the
panel and the command line run the same pass for one user. Only one pass
per user runs at a time, across processes; a second one is refused, not
queued.

@{h2}Where the archive lies

@{code}
data/session_archive/<user>/index.json           one entry per archived tree
data/session_archive/<user>/<YYYY-MM>/<root>.zip  the sessions, one JSON each
@{body}

The month is the conversation's own last change. Each zip also carries a
@{tt}_manifest.json@{utt} with the tree's sessions and the conversation's
title, agent and dates, so an archive stays readable without the index.
A restore deletes the zip after the sessions are back; a delete removes
the index entry and the zip. Files ending in @{tt}.tmp@{utt} are passes
that were killed while writing; a later pass drops them once they are an
hour old.
@endnode

@node api "The HTTP routes"
@{h1}The HTTP routes

All under @{tt}/plugins/session_archive/@{utt} (the plugin instance's
name). Each route works on the archive of the requesting user -- the
login's user name, @{tt}anonymous@{utt} without one; no route takes a user
as a parameter. With login on, the plugin routes need a logged-in user
(the @{tt}plugin_security@{utt} route rules).

@{table}
Method and path | Answer
GET / | the panel
GET /archived | user_id, retention_days, sweep_enabled, archived: the entries, newest archive first
POST /archived/{id}/restore | session_id, restored (sessions), title
DELETE /archived/{id} | session_id, sessions (how many the archive held)
POST /sweep?dry_run=false | the pass's report
@{body}

An archived entry carries @{tt}session_id@{utt} (the conversation's),
@{tt}title@{utt}, @{tt}agent_name@{utt}, @{tt}llm_profile@{utt},
@{tt}created_at@{utt}, @{tt}updated_at@{utt}, @{tt}message_count@{utt},
@{tt}session_count@{utt}, @{tt}archived_at@{utt}, @{tt}archive@{utt} (the
zip, relative to the user's folder), @{tt}bytes_live@{utt} (the size
before) and @{tt}bytes@{utt} (the zip's size).

The sweep's report: @{tt}trees@{utt} and @{tt}sessions@{utt} archived,
@{tt}bytes_live@{utt}, @{tt}bytes_archived@{utt}, @{tt}skipped_young@{utt}
(trees not old enough), @{tt}skipped_busy@{utt} (old enough, in use),
@{tt}capped@{utt} (the pass stopped at @{tt}max_trees_per_sweep@{utt}),
@{tt}remaining@{utt} (old enough and still in the store: the cap's rest,
the busy ones and the failed ones), @{tt}roots@{utt} (the conversations
taken) and @{tt}errors@{utt}. With @{tt}dry_run=true@{utt} it only counts,
writes nothing and is never capped; the panel does not offer that.

@{h2}Errors

@{table}
Status | When
404 | restore or delete of a conversation that is not in this user's archive
409 | a restore that cannot be done (see the panel node), a pass already running, the archive index held by another process
503 | the app runs without the archive service
@{body}

The reason is in @{tt}detail@{utt}, readable as it stands.
@endnode

@node cli "On the command line"
@{h1}On the command line

@{tt}agent-cli run@{utt} reaches the same archive; @{tt}--session-user@{utt}
says whose (without it, @{tt}cli_user@{utt}):

@{code bash}
agent-cli run --session-user ada --list-archived        # the 20 newest, 0 = all
agent-cli run --session-user ada --archive-sessions --dry-run
agent-cli run --session-user ada --archive-sessions 60  # older than 60 days
agent-cli run --session-user ada --restore-session <id>
@{body}

@{tt}--archive-sessions@{utt} without a number uses the configured
retention. A pass started here is refused while one runs in the API for the
same user, and the other way round; a dry run is never refused. See
@{" What moves, and when " link sweep} for what a command-line process can
and cannot see of running sessions.
@endnode

@node setup "Setting it up"
@{h1}Setting it up

The panel is a plugin, on in the default configuration:

@{code yaml}
plugins:
  servers:
    session_archive:
      type: session_archive
      enabled: true
@{body}

The archive and its sweep are part of the app, set in
@{tt}config/config.yaml@{utt}; this is the shipped block:

@{code yaml}
session_archive:
  enabled: true
  retention_days: 30
  sweep_interval_hours: 24.0
  first_sweep_delay_seconds: 300.0
  max_trees_per_sweep: 0
  # archive_path: null
@{body}

@{table}
Key | Default | Meaning
enabled | true | run the periodic sweep; the panel and Archive now work either way
retention_days | 30 | the age every session of a tree must reach; at least 1
sweep_interval_hours | 24.0 | the time between two periodic sweeps
first_sweep_delay_seconds | 300.0 | the wait after the start before the first one
max_trees_per_sweep | 0 | the most trees one pass archives; 0 means no cap
archive_path | session_archive in the data directory | where the zips and index lie
@{body}

Changes need a restart. The plugin has no settings of its own and no
dependencies beyond the app.
@endnode
